WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.920
With the ever-expanding narrative of the Marvel
Cinematic Universe comes the burden of mystery.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.019
There are many unanswered questions about
the MCU, some of which have simmered on the

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.820
back burner since almost the very beginning.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.480
Here are a few of the biggest lingering questions.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.600
Both Ant-Man films offer some of the lightest,
most comedy-centered fare in the MCU, so it

00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:22.070
would be understandable if the kinds of fans
who always have their ears and eyes open for

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.300
potential "secrets" let their guard down and
missed a couple of things.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.179
But with the release of Ant-Man and The Wasp,
one of the biggest unanswered questions of

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:34.020
the MCU may have something to do with who
the new Big Bad of the MCU will be after Thanos

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.200
is finally defeated.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:40.500
Early in the film, both Wasp and Ghost easily
dispatch the thugs of the ruthless Sonny Burch,

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:44.460
yet Burch just keeps coming back because he's
working for someone who doesn't take no for

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:44.960
an answer.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.020
"Really?

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:47.840
This guy again?"

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.239
In case you were too busy laughing and didn't
notice, Burch never reveals exactly who it

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.180
is he's working for.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.280
He mentions his employer to Wasp and we hear
him talking on the phone with them, but we

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:57.940
never get a name.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.480
Obviously, it's too early to tell.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.050
It may be that director Peyton Reed was simply
setting up the villain for the third Ant-Man

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:04.540
film.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.980
But considering the possibilities of Hank
Pym's quantum technology, whoever's hunting

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.600
for it probably isn't looking to rob banks
and knock over drug stores.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.140
A heavy hitter is coming for Pym's tech, and
they might occupy Thanos' empty chief villain

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.540
throne after Avengers 4.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.320
In Avengers: Infinity War, we learn that Thanos
captured the first of the Infinity Stones

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.040
off-camera: the Power Stone, also known as
the Orb.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.350
We see Nova Prime put the Orb in a vault on
Xandar in the last few minutes of Guardians

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.020
of the Galaxy.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:35.160
The Mad Titan already has the stone when he
and the Black Order attack the Asgardian refugee

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.360
ship in Infinity War, and later in the film,
we learn that Thanos "decimated" Xandar.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.100
But the word "decimate" isn't always used
with precision, particularly not when it comes

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:44.640
to Thanos.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.160
We know his usual way of doing things is to
wipe out half the population of a planet.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.140
If half the population of Earth were wiped
out, you could use the word "decimate" without

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.340
sounding too dramatic.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.800
So did Thanos and the Black Order literally
destroy the whole population?

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.520
Or did they just bring it to its knees?

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.260
Is it possible we might see Nova Prime or
Corpsman Dey again?

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.480
Whatever happens, it would seem strange to
not have the events on Xandar trigger more

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.840
events further down the road.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.700
It may even be the narrative tool Marvel Studios
uses to bring the hero Nova into the MCU.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.240
Sif was not only missing from Avengers: Infinity
War, but she was out of action for Thor: Ragnarok

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:19.740
as well.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.520
According to actress Jaime Alexander, she
didn't show up for the third Thor because

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.680
of a scheduling conflict with her NBC action
series Blindspot.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.180
Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige gave
a narrative explanation that leaves the door

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.800
open for Sif's return, saying Loki would want
Thor's closest allies far away from him.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.360
Just as we find out from Skurge in the beginning
of Ragnarok that Heimdall was falsely accused

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.740
of treason, Loki probably also used his Odin
disguise to get rid of Sif.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.660
Feige went so far as to speculate that the
God of Mischief had her banished.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.120
If Sif wasn't on Asgard when Surtur destroyed
it at the end of Ragnarok, then she wasn't

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.540
on the refugee ship either.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.330
That means Sif could still be alive out there
in the cosmos, waiting to hear back from a

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.380
world that's long gone and a people who aren't
doing much better.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.840
Strangely, a term we haven't heard used at
all in the MCU yet is "Elders of the Universe,"

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.440
in spite of the fact that three of the Elders
from the comics have appeared in the films.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.580
One of the elders from Marvel Comics, Ego
the Living Planet, was a major villain in

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.440
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:21.640
In the comics, the Elders are a group of powerful
cosmic beings who share three common traits:

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.700
each is powerful, each is the last surviving
member of their respective race, and each

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.820
is obsessed with a specific pursuit.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.550
For some it's collecting, for others it's
gaming, gardening, or in some cases, just

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:33.800
good, old-fashioned killing.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.120
Though neither the Collector nor the Grandmaster
have been shown to have any overt relationship

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.090
to one another in the films, in the comics
they're both counted among the Elders.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.900
Physically, they've both been given similar
marks on their chins.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:49.000
It may be that they're both meant to be related
to another in some way, but that it's just

00:03:49.001 --> 00:03:52.560
a footnote for the fans, not something that
will ever matter that much in the overall

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.760
story of the MCU.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.540
But there are reasons to think the two could
be much more connected than we think.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.780
Both potentially have some kind of relationship
with Loki.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.680
Remember that it was Loki disguised as Odin
who sent Volstagg and Sif to deliver the Aether

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.540
to the Collector in the mid-credits scene
of Thor: The Dark World.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.980
And in Thor: Ragnarok, we never learned exactly
how Loki won Grandmaster's favor.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:17.640
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 gave us the
answer to the origin of Peter Quill.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:21.190
We learned who his father was, and how that
lineage allowed Quill to hold an Infinity

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:22.360
Stone without dying.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.500
It was good to get answers about Peter, but
there's another Guardian whose origins remain

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:27.540
shrouded in mystery.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.940
We know very little about Rocket.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.420
We know he's the product of scientific experiments.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.350
We know the Nova Corps refers to him as

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.520
"Subject 89P13.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:38.620
Calls itself Rocket."

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.300
in the first Guardians film.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.400
And of course we know Rocket isn't exactly
thrilled about the nature of his existence.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.260
We don't know the identities of the scientists
who made him, why they did what they did,

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.540
or how Rocket escaped them.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.420
Guardians writer and director James Gunn has
said he doesn't plan to stick to Rocket's

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:57.520
comic book origin, but even with that in mind,
we still don't know exactly what direction

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.520
he's going with the story.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.600
The second and final season of Netflix's Iron
Fist ends with a tease.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.560
Danny Rand and Ward Meachum are globetrotting
together to find a man named Orson Randall.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.500
In the comics, Orson is an Iron Fist who turned
his back on his duties to protect the sacred

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.520
city K'un-Lun.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.860
The Netflix series ends with the reveal that
Danny Rand acquired Randall's pistols, which

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.100
appear to be infused with Iron Fist energy.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.000
Though Randall's pistols don't exactly have
special abilities in the comics, the scene

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.000
is a clear reference to those stories.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.950
Unfortunately, we may never know what happens
to Danny.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:32.920
In October 2018, Iron Fist became the first
of the Netflix Marvel series to get the axe.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:37.120
Danny Rand's travels are briefly mentioned
in the final season of Jessica Jones, but

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.940
not in a way that provides closure or any
details.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.260
Perhaps Disney could revive the show someday,
but if they do it won't be for a while.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:49.540
Avengers: Endgame brought the Gamora from
2014 into the present, filling Star-Lord with

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:53.240
the hope that he could have the love of his
life back in spite of her untimely death during

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:53.860
Infinity War.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:58.640
Unfortunately, this Gamora has never met Quill
and their first meeting doesn't exactly go

00:05:58.640 --> 00:05:59.140
well.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.040
She's nowhere in sight after the battle with
Thanos and his armies, leading some to wonder

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.840
if Tony Stark's snap unintentionally killed
her along with her adoptive father.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:11.110
We know now that a scene was filmed with Gamora
after the battle, showing she did survive

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.060
Tony's snap, but it was deleted.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.820
So where is Gamora, and what are her plans?

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:20.760
Shortly after Endgame's release we made some
educated guesses, but it's still just speculation.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:24.920
At this point we can only guess that Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 3 will be the film that

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.760
finally sheds some light on what happened
to her.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:28.160
"Where's Gamora?"

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.020
"Yeah, I'll do you one better.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:31.180
Who's Gamora?"

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:32.980
"I'll do you one better!

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:34.540
Why's Gamora?"

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:39.100
The fact that this is a Gamora from the past
also fills her return with a lot of potential.

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:42.080
This new/old Gamora could go in any direction.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.660
She could rejoin the Guardians, she could
become a villain, or something in between.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.730
This is a Gamora who never met Peter Quill
and who never fought alongside him and the

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:50.580
other Guardians.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:51.960
Who knows who she'll become?

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:57.120
In 2016's Captain America: Civil War, Bucky
Barnes is treated like the most dangerous

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:58.040
person alive.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.340
With enhanced abilities and skills that make
him a match for Captain America, a metal arm

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.040
strong enough to tear through Iron Man's armor,
and mental conditioning that can turn him

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.860
into a killing machine, Bucky is a potential
time bomb.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:13.320
By the end of the film, T'Challa agrees to
keep him in cryogenic stasis in Wakanda.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:17.860
By the end of Black Panther we see Bucky is
unfrozen and living among T'Challa's people,

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.120
which suggests he's made quite a bit of progress.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:24.580
Strangely, we have yet to get a confirmation
that Shuri was able to erase Bucky's mental

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.080
triggers.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.200
When the paralyzed Everett Ross is brought
to Wakanda for her help in Black Panther,

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:32.500
Shuri briefly mentions helping another "white
boy," but that's it.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.780
We've yet to hear any definitive word on whether
or not Bucky can still be turned into a weapon

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.880
with that list of random phrases Zemo finds
in Civil War.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.660
Considering we know Zemo is set to return
in the Falcon and the Winter Soldier miniseries,

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:47.300
it could be that Shuri was never able to complete
her task.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.040
Daniel Brühl may even have hinted this is
the case when, during the 2019 San Diego Comic-Con,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.640
he posted a first look of himself as Zemo
for the new series on Instagram.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.560
In the caption for the photo, Brühl added
the ten-phrase pass code that turns Bucky

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.820
back into the Winter Soldier in Civil War.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.760
Carol Danvers ended up being pivotal in the
battle against Thanos.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:11.220
She saves Tony Stark, destroys Thanos' ship
Sanctuary before it can kill the other heroes,

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.800
and in a one-on-one fight, she proves to be
very capable against the Mad Titan.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:18.200
In spite of her importance, she's gone for
most of Avengers: Endgame.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:22.800
While communicating with Black Widow via holographic
image, she lets her know not to expect her

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.420
for a while, and then she's gone until the
final battle.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:29.910
We probably won't know until Captain Marvel
2 where exactly the hero spent her time for

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:33.850
most of Endgame, but a good possibility is
that she was trying to stop everyone else

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:35.720
in the galaxy from killing one another.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.560
"There are a lot of other planets in the universe,
and unfortunately they didn't have you guys."

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:44.960
While we get to see how the Snap impacts Earth,
we have yet to see how the rest of the galaxy

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.100
responds to losing half its population.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.420
It's possible, if not likely, that when it
comes to some of the interstellar empires

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:55.540
out there in the cosmos, there are a lot of
power shifts in the wake of Thanos' snap.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:59.600
After all, most of the galaxy has no way of
knowing in the immediate aftermath why half

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.340
its people simply disappeared.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:05.790
Confusion is probably reigning all over the
spaceways after the conclusion of Infinity

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.660
War, and that's probably what Captain Marvel
is dealing with.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:10.760
That said, we still don't know for sure.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.620
The aftermath of Infinity War is so drastic
that one of the biggest changes in the MCU

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.740
in the years before Thanos' snap seems utterly
forgotten by the time of Avengers: Endgame.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:24.500
There's no mention of the world-changing laws
known as The Sokovia Accords in either Endgame

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:25.960
or Spider-Man: Far from Home.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.400
That means no one has clarified whether \or
not international law still requires the Avengers

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.420
to operate under the watch of the United Nations.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:37.280
After five years, Captain America is regularly
visiting the Avengers compound and attending

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:40.320
a support group, so it doesn't seem like he's
a fugitive anymore.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.770
Ant-Man's in the Quantum Realm, Hawkeye's
gone rogue, and the rest of Captain America:

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:48.320
Civil War's anti-registration heroes are erased
by Thanos' snap.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:52.700
It seems clear that after the end of Infinity
War either the Sokovia Accords were repealed

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:54.800
or that no one was willing to enforce them.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:56.440
Either possibility makes sense.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:00.560
After all, Bruce Banner is allowed to roam
free and take selfies in diners even though

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.840
the last time he was on Earth he was wanted
for destroying half a city.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:08.570
Regardless, we never learn exactly why Cap
is allowed to roam free or what the UN thinks

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.660
about the Avengers' new talking raccoon and
blue-skinned cyborg.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:16.460
Taneleer Tivan, also known as The Collector,
remains one of the most mysterious figures

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:17.480
in the MCU.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:22.000
Adding to his mystery is the simple fact that
we have no idea whether or not he's alive.

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:25.800
While we see the Collector in Infinity War,
it isn't actually him.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.500
When we see Thanos torturing the Collector
for the location of the Reality Stone, it's

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.060
an illusion the Titan creates to fool the
Guardians.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:35.360
The Collector is long gone by the time the
Guardians arrive on Knowhere.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:39.780
Thanos has already captured the Stone and
Tivan's famous collection is ablaze.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:43.580
Thanos might have killed the Collector before
the Guardians arrived, but it's also possible

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.740
that the obsessive Tivan escaped and leaves
left the Reality Stone behind.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:50.780
Without the Stone, Tivan meant nothing to
the Titan, so perhaps The Collector decided

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.030
to ditch the valuables and escape with his
life.

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:54.030
We just don't know at this point.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.580
We don't get to see what happens to Mjolnir
in the resolution of Avengers: Endgame.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:02.380
Captain America has the hammer with him when
Hulk sends him back in time, but it isn't

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:04.620
there with him when he reappears as an elderly
man.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:09.400
Presumably, he would have returned the hammer
to Asgard circa 2013 along with the Aether.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.420
That was clearly the intended mission.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.270
Otherwise, he would have left the Thor of
that time hammerless when he still had Malekith

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:16.820
and the Dark Elf army to deal with.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:18.500
Still, we don't see it happen.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:22.240
It's doubtful that Steve would be selfish
enough to keep Mjolnir for himself.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:24.040
But it's possible something went wrong.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:28.140
Did Steve find the Thor of that time and hand
Mjolnir to him, or did he just leave it standing

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:31.420
on the ground somewhere, trusting that it
would answer Thor's call when needed?

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:35.140
Now that we know Jane Foster is coming back
for Thor: Love and Thunder, and that she's

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:38.720
somehow going to gain the power of Thor, the
question of what happened to the hammer seems

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:39.720
more important.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:43.240
It could be that whatever method Steve chooses
chose to return the hammer is what will lead

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.740
to Foster becoming the MCU's new thunder god.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:50.600
Spider-Man: Far from Home's post-credits scene
has inspired a lot of fan speculation about

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.040
who we've been calling "Nick Fury."

00:11:52.589 --> 00:11:56.680
The scene reveals that for at least part of
Far from Home, if not longer, the real Nick

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:57.960
Fury wasn't even on Earth.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.820
Talos the Skrull replaced Fury while Soren
replaced Maria Hill.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:06.080
In the meantime, the real Fury is shown on
some kind of space station or remote facility

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:07.500
with a bunch of other Skrulls.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:11.380
The first and most obvious question is how
long Talos and Soren have been playing their

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:11.960
roles.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:16.220
Since the Skrulls are introduced in Captain
Marvel, which is set in the '90s, it's possible

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:19.600
Talos and Soren have been disguised as Fury
and Hill for multiple films.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:24.660
Talos could have been disguised as Fury as
early as 2015's Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:28.620
That's his first appearance after Captain
America: The Winter Soldier, and it's doubtful

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:32.649
that film's Fury could've been a Skrull, because
Talos probably wouldn't be able to retain

00:12:32.649 --> 00:12:35.000
his disguise after being nearly killed by
Bucky.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:39.520
There's another burning question, too: What
exactly is Fury working on with the Skrulls?

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:43.900
Talos and his brethren likely feel indebted
to Fury after he helps save them from the

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.200
Kree, but that doesn't tell us what they're
doing with him.

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:49.580
Speaking of the Skrulls we know have been
hiding in plain sight, what about the ones

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:50.920
we don't know about?

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:55.890
Fans have speculated for a long time about
which MCU characters could be replaced by

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:59.580
Skrulls, and it turned out that Nick Fury
was officially one of those characters.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:03.760
But that Spider-Man: Far from Home post-credits
scene did more than reveal that both Fury

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:05.840
and Maria Hill were replaced by Skrulls.

00:13:06.149 --> 00:13:09.480
It also showed us the Skrulls have been working
with Fury for a while.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.990
And if that's the case, then what are the
odds this is the only time Fury's asked them

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.500
to use their shape-changing abilities for
his benefit?

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:17.400
They don't seem terribly high.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:22.380
If that's the case, then there are likely
more Skrulls out there on MCU's Earth, strategically

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:24.100
placed and disguised as regular humans.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.890
If you saw Spider-Man: Far from Home, then
you know it gave us the return of a very minor

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:31.060
character from 2008's Iron Man.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:35.910
William Ginter Riva, a scientist working for
Stark Industries in Iron Man, returns in a

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:37.720
very different way in the Spidey sequel.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:42.280
In Far from Home, Quentin Beck recruits Riva
as part of a small army of disgruntled Stark

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:42.860
employees.

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:45.480
Unlike Beck, Riva survives the events of the
film.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.720
And he apparently does more than survive.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.800
In a mid-credits scene, we learn that an altered
video has been released which seems to prove

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:56.300
that Spider-Man murdered Quentin Beck, but
not before Beck could reveal Spidey's true

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:56.800
identity.

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:01.260
Since Riva was recording the events remotely,
he's likely the person who fed the doctored

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:02.900
video to J. Jonah Jameson.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:06.540
"Spider-Man's name is Peter Parker!"

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:10.320
If Riva is the one who sets up Spidey, we
have to wonder why.

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:14.480
Is Riva just trying to get back at Spider-Man,
or does he have something bigger planned?

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:18.930
He doesn't come off as a guy with the stiffest
backbone in the MCU, so it doesn't seem like

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.780
he's gunning to be Mysterio's successor.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:23.500
Maybe Riva didn't release the information
to the public.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.300
Maybe he sold it to someone else we haven't
met yet.

